<%# Fuime: the arithmetic lesson.

    The worked example below carries real figures, and that is deliberate. The
    §8.3 D2 rule forbids Fuime SUGGESTING what to charge; it does not forbid
    arithmetic, and a break-even lesson with no numbers in it teaches nothing.
    The figures are one fictional operator's COSTS, labelled as an illustration,
    and the price in the example is presented as Maya's decision rather than as a
    rate anybody should copy. Keep it that way if you edit this. %>

<p>
  Four words do most of the work in a small business: revenue, costs, margin and
  break even. If you understand these you can tell whether a business works
  before you have run it for a year.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Revenue is not profit</h2>

<p>
  Revenue is everything customers paid you. Profit is what is left after the
  business has paid for itself. Confusing the two is how a busy month feels
  brilliant and leaves you with nothing.
</p>

<p>Costs come in two shapes, and the difference matters more than it sounds:</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    <strong>Variable costs</strong> happen per job. Materials, postage, fuel.
    Sell twice as much and you pay twice as much.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Fixed costs</strong> happen whether you sell anything or not. A
    subscription, a domain, a tool you rent monthly. These get cheaper per job
    the busier you are, which is why a business with high fixed costs is
    dangerous when it is quiet and very good when it is busy.
  </li>
</ul>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Margin: what one sale is really worth</h2>

<p>
  Take the price of one sale and subtract only the variable costs of that sale.
  What is left is your margin, sometimes called contribution. It is the amount
  each sale contributes towards your fixed costs, and then towards profit.
</p>

<p>
  Margin is the number to improve. You can raise it by charging more, by buying
  materials better, or by getting faster. Selling more units does not raise it at
  all, which is why "just sell more" is not a fix for a business with thin
  margins. It only makes you busier at the same result.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <h2 class="mt-0 mb-2 text-lg">Worked example</h2>
  <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-sm">
    Maya prints and posts sticker packs. These figures are made up to show the
    arithmetic. Yours will be completely different, and the price is hers, not a
    recommendation.
  </p>
  <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-sm">
    Maya sells a pack for $8. Each pack costs her $1.40 in paper and ink, $0.30
    in packaging and $1.10 in postage. That is $2.80 of variable cost, so her
    margin is <strong>$5.20 a pack</strong>.
  </p>
  <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-sm">
    She pays $15 a month for the design software she uses, and nothing else
    fixed. So she needs $15 divided by $5.20, which is 2.9, rounded up to
    <strong>three packs a month</strong> before the business has paid for
    itself. Everything after the third pack is profit.
  </p>
  <p class="m-0 text-sm">
    Now the important part. Maya spends about 20 minutes packing and posting each
    pack. Ten packs is over three hours of work for $52 of margin, before she has
    counted the time spent designing them. That is the real question her numbers
    are asking, and no amount of selling more packs answers it. Charging more,
    posting in batches, or selling a bigger pack does.
  </p>
</div>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">When one more copy costs you nothing</h2>

<p>
  Software and digital products break the arithmetic above in one specific way:
  the variable cost is almost zero. Selling a template to a second person costs
  you nothing. Ten more people using your tool might cost you a few cents of
  hosting.
</p>

<p>That changes four things, and they are worth knowing before you start.</p>

<ul class="m-0 pl-5 flex flex-col gap-1">
  <li>
    <strong>Almost the whole price is margin.</strong> So the question stops
    being "what does each one cost me" and becomes "how many people want this".
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Your real costs are fixed, not variable.</strong> Hosting, domains,
    AI usage and any tool you pay for monthly. Those bills arrive whether you sell
    anything or not, which is why a quiet month hurts a software business more
    than it hurts a lawn business.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>Pricing by effort is the wrong instinct.</strong> Nobody cares that
    it took you three weekends. They care what it saves them. Two things that took
    the same time to build are worth completely different amounts.
  </li>
  <li>
    <strong>All the risk moves to the front.</strong> With mowing you find out in
    an afternoon whether anyone will pay. With software you can spend two months
    building before you learn anything at all, which is why
    <%= link_to "testing the idea first", learn_lesson_path(slug: "what-people-pay-for", venture: venture&.slug), class: "underline" %>
    matters so much more here.
  </li>
</ul>

<p>
  Break even still works the same way, you just measure it against those fixed
  costs. If your hosting and tools come to a certain amount each month, that
  divided by your price is how many sales you need before the thing pays for
  itself.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Break even is a target, not a worry</h2>

<p>
  Fixed costs divided by margin per sale gives you the number of sales you need
  each month before you make anything. Work it out once. It turns a vague anxiety
  into a number you can count towards.
</p>

<p>
  It also tells you something uncomfortable and useful. If the answer is more
  sales than you could physically deliver in a month, the problem is not effort.
  It is the price, or it is the fixed costs, and no amount of working harder will
  fix either.
</p>

<h2 class="text-lg font-bold mt-2 mb-0">Count your own time even though nobody invoices you for it</h2>

<p>
  This is the mistake almost every first business makes. Leave your hours out and
  a job looks profitable when it is actually paying you less than a normal
  weekend job would. You are allowed to decide that is fine, because you are
  learning something or building towards something. You are not allowed to not
  know.
</p>

<div class="card">
  <p class="m-0 text-sm">
    <strong>Do this today:</strong> take your last sale. Write down the price,
    everything that sale cost you, and honestly how long it took including
    travel. That single calculation will tell you more about your business than
    any advice will.
  </p>
</div>
